{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And all the work was completed, etc.</b> That is, they did not complete it — for had HaShem commanded yet more in His service, blessed be He, the children of Israel would have performed it with all the desire of their soul — but rather the work that HaShem had commanded was completed." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>was completed</b>, that there were in it works that were made on their own — namely the menorah, concerning which they of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 15) that it was made on its own. Therefore Scripture did not ascribe everything to them, for there is a portion of the work that was not made except by striking with a hammer, and it was completed on its own." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes along the lines of the verse (Psalms 84:3): ״נִכְסְפָה וְגַם כָלְתָה נַפְשִׁי לְחַצְרוֹת ה׳״ — for they performed all the work with longing and consummate beauty." "<b>And the children of Israel made, etc.</b> And although it was only Bezalel and the wise of heart who made it — a person's emissary is like himself, and it is as though all Israel made it. And although we do not find that Bezalel was appointed except by the word of HaShem and not that they sent him, even so, they did consent to him." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that here Scripture makes a statement about the principle of collective fulfillment in the observance of Torah, and shows that the children of Israel merit for one another, and the Torah was given to be fulfilled in the collective of Israel — each one does what is in his power and they merit for one another. And perhaps this is what is alluded to in the verse (Leviticus 19:18): ״וְאָהַבְתָּ לְרֵעֲךָ כָּמוֹךָ״ — meaning, because he is like you, for through his well-being it is good for you and through his agency you complete your perfection; and if so, he is not another but you yourself, like one of your own limbs." "And with this we find satisfaction of spirit, for HaShem commanded six hundred and thirteen commandments, and it is impossible that a single person be found who stands in the fulfillment of all of them. And this is your sign: priest, Levite, Israelite, and women — there are positive commandments among the priests for which there is no possibility for an Israelite to perform, and there are commandments for Israelites that do not apply among the priests, and so among the Levites, and so among the women. And what possibility does an individual have to fulfill them, to complete the rectification for his 248 limbs and his 365 sinews which correspond to them? Rather, it is certain that the Torah is fulfilled through the totality of the congregation, and they merit from one another. And this is what the verse says here: <b>and the children of Israel did according to all that HaShem had commanded</b> — it attributes to all of them together the deed of all of them, even though some of them brought the donation, some did the labor; it says concerning their totality that they did everything. And it placed this statement adjacent to the making of the garments, which individuals from Israel made, in order to inform you that even concerning this particular it says that the children of Israel made it with no distinction. And from now on every one of Israel brought all thirteen species that were in the donation and performed all the labors that are mentioned." "<b>According to all, etc.</b> It intends by this to remove suspicion from them, for a beholder who sees that HaShem commanded concerning the Tabernacle and it was made, yet they folded it up for three months (Exodus Rabbah 52:2), will say that there was a cause on the side of the deed — that there was something unfitting in it — or that Israel held back something from all that is stated in its commandment; therefore it says <b>and was completed</b>, etc., <b>and the children of Israel did according to all</b>. And what was the reason the Holy One, blessed be He, waited until the first month, which is the praiseworthy month called ״first,״ as He said at the completion of the work (Exodus 40:2): ״בְּיוֹם הַחֹדֶשׁ הָרִאשׁוֹן תָּקִים״, etc.? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah ad loc.) because Abraham was informed of Isaac." "<b>that He commanded, etc., so they did.</b> The reason for His saying <b>they did</b> — and He did not suffice with the statement ״and they did״ — is perhaps that the verse credits them with having fulfilled everything that HaShem commanded Moses in the Torah, and this is the verse's measure: <b>and the children of Israel did according to all</b>, etc., as stated in the matter; and by this the verse reckons them as though all that HaShem commanded Moses — as in all the Torah — so they did. And with this the ashes of the sin of the Calf were burned and scattered."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:35.192209606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}